  "body": "This answers #306's cost by writing less. It is stacked on John's return-chain candidate (`probe/ring-return-chain`, 88e215ed) and lands after it.\n\nBLAKE3-Servil mt's stream is limited by its producer at short messages: the workers spend about a third of their cycles waiting. Each task is 1,656 bytes. The stream built every task fresh, writing all 64 members, and `push` copied all 1,656 bytes into the ring. A task of four 4 KiB messages uses 312 of them.\n\nNow the stream keeps its open task and resets only the header as a task opens. `push` writes the header and the members the task holds into its slot, and each slot holds an initialized task from the start. `publish`, `publish_part`, `push` and `run_task` take the task by reference.\n\nOn the i7-12700K against the ring integration 0df4fa7e, the stream is faster in every pair: 64 B -4%, 128 B -6% to -8%, 4 KiB -9%, 16 KiB -14%; no cell is slower over 20 points and the pipeline contender. On 88e215ed, against fbfb319f (before the ring fix), no cell is slower and 4 KiB to 16 KiB are 7% to 13% faster. Each half earns its place: keeping the task gives the short messages, the shorter copy the 4 KiB ones. [Data](https://github.com/devonjonte/bench-hashes/tree/data/2026-11-08/task-prefix-i7).\n\nChecks: 227 tests, 21 doc examples, Shuttle, `check-builds.sh`, and a pipeline loop. The mutation check finds 13 mutants and no new survivor. `a_push_writes_the_members_a_task_holds_alone` fails with a whole-array copy, and a new test covers `Source::help`.\n\nJohn, it needs the Mac's check, the GPU path's `take_run` above all.\n\n\u2014 Devon Jonte\n",
